# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# edge-gateway configuration
# Copy to `.env`, fill in, then:  docker compose up -d
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Email used for Let's Encrypt registration / expiry notices.
ACME_EMAIL=admin@example.com

# ── Optional Traefik dashboard ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave DASHBOARD_HOST empty to disable. Generate DASHBOARD_AUTH with:
#   htpasswd -nb admin 'your-password'
# In this file, DO NOT escape the $ signs (values are read literally).
DASHBOARD_HOST=
DASHBOARD_AUTH=

# ── Routes (file provider) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ROUTE_* is ONLY for apps that do NOT carry their own Traefik labels.
#
# Apps that already ship Traefik labels are discovered automatically by the
# Docker provider — do NOT add them here. In particular a multi-tenant app that
# already ships Traefik labels needs NO ROUTE_* entry: its tenant containers
# attach to their own shared data-plane network with per-tenant routing/middleware
# labels and the edge picks them up on its own. See README "How a labelled
# multi-tenant app plugs in".
#
# One block per label-less app. Number them 1,2,3,... contiguously (a gap stops
# parsing).
#
#   ROUTE_N_NAME         unique id (also the Traefik router/service name)
#   ROUTE_N_HOSTS        comma-separated hostnames for this app
#   ROUTE_N_SERVICE      backend url — container on the `web` network, or external
#   ROUTE_N_TLS          optional, default true (HTTPS + auto cert)
#   ROUTE_N_ENTRYPOINTS  optional, default websecure

# Every block below is COMMENTED OUT on purpose: a fresh `cp .env.example .env`
# should bring the edge up with no routes at all, not with a route pointing at a
# container that does not exist. Uncomment and edit what you actually need.

# Example: a plain Docker app on the `web` network with no Traefik labels
# ROUTE_1_NAME=dashboard
# ROUTE_1_HOSTS=dash.example.com
# ROUTE_1_SERVICE=http://dashboard-app:8080

# Example: an app that lives outside Docker / on another host
# ROUTE_2_NAME=legacy
# ROUTE_2_HOSTS=legacy.example.com
# ROUTE_2_SERVICE=http://10.0.0.42:9000

# ── Static SPAs (dockerized edge-spa nginx) ──────────────────────────────────
# APP_* is for NON-dockerized frontend builds: the edge-spa nginx container serves
# them from the host's /var/www (bind-mounted read-only), and Traefik routes to it
# by container name. One block per app; number 1,2,3,... contiguously.
#
#   APP_N_NAME         unique id (router/service names derive from it)
#   APP_N_WEB_HOSTS    comma-separated hostnames for the web build
#   APP_N_WEB_ROOT     on-host build dir under /var/www (served by edge-spa)
#   APP_N_ADMIN_HOSTS  optional — hostnames for a separate admin build
#   APP_N_ADMIN_ROOT   optional — its build dir (required if ADMIN_HOSTS is set)
#   APP_N_API          optional — backend url the API prefixes are proxied to
#                      (adds an API router + CORS). A container on `web`, or a
#                      non-dockerized host process via http://host.docker.internal:<port>
#   APP_N_API_PATHS    optional, default '/api,/socket.io' — the path prefixes
#                      routed to APP_N_API instead of to the SPA. Set this when
#                      the frontend calls a different prefix; each entry must
#                      start with '/'.
#
# Example:
# APP_1_NAME=example-app
# APP_1_WEB_HOSTS=example.com,www.example.com
# APP_1_WEB_ROOT=/var/www/example-app/web/html
# APP_1_ADMIN_HOSTS=admin.example.com,www.admin.example.com
# APP_1_ADMIN_ROOT=/var/www/example-app/admin/html
# APP_1_API=http://api-backend:8080
# APP_1_API_PATHS=/api,/socket.io

# ── Raw-TCP backends (non-HTTP protocols, e.g. MongoDB) ──────────────────────
# TCP_* routes a non-HTTP protocol through the edge on a DEDICATED entrypoint.
# A protocol like Mongo's sends no cleartext SNI, so it cannot share :443 with the
# web traffic — it needs a port of its own. Number 1,2,3,... contiguously.
#
#   TCP_N_NAME        unique id (also the Traefik router/service name)
#   TCP_N_ENTRYPOINT  entrypoint name — must exist in the STATIC config
#   TCP_N_SERVICE     backend as host:port (NOT a url, unlike ROUTE_N_SERVICE)
#   TCP_N_HOSTSNI     optional, default '*' (any SNI, plaintext passthrough)
#   TCP_N_TLS         optional, default true — Traefik terminates TLS with a
#                     Let's Encrypt cert and forwards plaintext to the backend
#   TCP_N_ALLOWLIST   optional, comma-separated CIDRs allowed to connect
#
# TLS and HOSTSNI are coupled: TLS=true needs a real hostname to get a cert for,
# and Traefik rejects a non-wildcard HostSNI on a plaintext router. So it's either
# (TLS=true + a hostname) or (TLS=false + HOSTSNI='*'); the generator rejects the
# other two combinations.
#
# An entrypoint CANNOT be declared here — it is Traefik static config, which is
# read from exactly one file. Each server declares its own in
# $RUNTIME_CONFIG_DIR/traefik.override.yml (copy traefik/traefik.override.example.yml)
# and publishes the port from its docker-compose.override.yml.
#
# ⚠ Without TCP_N_ALLOWLIST the backend is reachable from the entire internet. For
# a database that is a matter of hours — set it to the addresses you connect from.
# For occasional access an SSH tunnel exposes nothing at all and is the safer tool.
#
# Example: MongoDB for a desktop client, mongod being a host process on :27017
# TCP_1_NAME=mongo
# TCP_1_ENTRYPOINT=mongo
# TCP_1_SERVICE=host.docker.internal:27017
# TCP_1_HOSTSNI=mongo.example.com
# TCP_1_TLS=true
# TCP_1_ALLOWLIST=203.0.113.7/32
#
# Compass then connects with:
#   mongodb://<user>:<pass>@mongo.example.com:27018/?tls=true&directConnection=true
